<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arabah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wadi Araba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Arabah is the long desert valley running south from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aqaba">Gulf of Aqaba</a>, now forming part of the border between Israel and Jordan. Rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> ores at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faynan">Faynan</a> and Timna, it was an important mining area in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Ages</a>, and the land of Edom lay in the mountains on its eastern side. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">Caravan routes</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> also crossed it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arabah.jpg" alt="Arabah" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: בתיה בן צבי · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PikiWiki_Israel_8801_Plants_of_Israel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Arabah, also called Aravah or Wadi Araba, is a loosely defined area south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a> basin and north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aqaba">Gulf of Aqaba</a>, in south-eastern Israel and south-western Jordan. Until about the early 20th century the term covered almost the whole Jordan Rift Valley, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-galilee">Sea of Galilee</a> to the Gulf of Aqaba, including the Dead Sea itself. Today it names only the southern section, 166 kilometres long.</p><p>From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aqaba">Gulf of Aqaba</a> the land rises over 77 kilometres to 230 metres above sea level, the watershed between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. It then slopes gently north for 74 kilometres and drops steeply in the last stretch to the Dead Sea, 417 metres below sea level. The southern Arabah is hot, dry and almost without rain. Acacia savanna there supports Nubian ibex, Arabian oryx, Asiatic wild ass and acacia gazelles, preyed on by Arabian wolves, striped hyenas and golden jackals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Ages</a> the Arabah was a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> production. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> reports that King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> had mines there, and mining at the Ashalim site predates his reign in the 10th century BCE. The area, especially its eastern part, belonged to the Edomites, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idumaeans">Idumeans</a> in Hellenistic and Roman times, and later the eastern Arabah became the domain of the Nabateans, builders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a>.</p><p>In 2019 archaeologists led by Erez Ben-Yosef and Tom Levy argued that the biblical Kingdom of Edom existed, on the evidence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> samples from the Timna Valley and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faynan">Faynan</a> dated to 1300–800 BCE. They thought Pharaoh Shoshenk I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> encouraged copper trade and production rather than destroying the region. Ben-Yosef said the findings contradict the view that the Arabah was held by a loose alliance of tribes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Israel–Jordan Peace Treaty was signed in the Arabah on 26 October 1994. A plan to bring sea water from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a> along the valley was agreed to be built on the Jordanian side, and Jordan abandoned the project in 2021. In 2021 Eilat had 52,753 inhabitants and Aqaba 95,048, and twenty small settlements, mostly kibbutzim and moshavim, lie north of Eilat.</p><p>Five major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> tribes live in eight settlements on the Jordanian side, herding sheep, farming, making handicrafts and serving in the Jordanian army. Timna Valley Park has prehistoric rock carvings and some of the oldest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mines in the world, and Wadi Feynan on the Jordanian side holds the copper-mining site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khirbat-en-nahas">Khirbat en-Nahas</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arabah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Edom lay in the south of present-day Jordan, east of the <b>Arabah</b>, with the territory of Moab to its north; the Zered, now Wadi al-Hasa, marked the boundary between the two.</blockquote><a href="/empire/edom">Edom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Edomites lived in the mountains east of the <b>Arabah</b> valley, mentioned in Egyptian texts from the 13th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/edom">Edom</a></li></ul></section>
